  "raw": " types.\n\nPatients with blood group A are most at risk of life threatening blood-borne diseases as HIV and the blood-based cancers, haemophilia and the multiple myeloma are often passed on from mother to unborn children. Blood group A is also required due to the use of blood transfusions.\n\nIt is recommended, in blood group A , to be vaccinated for hepatitis B at age one year and the hepatitis B vaccine has been shown to prevent complications caused by HIV. TVNBS offers a weekly HIV screening programme for patients. TVNBS identified patients at high risk of HIV and paid for the first week’s treatment; this may be available for insurance claims for insurance purposes. TVNBS identified patients at high risk of HIV and paid for the first week’s treatment; this may be available for insurance claims for insurance purposes. TVNBS identified patients at high risk of HIV and paid for the first week’s treatment; this may",
